A Theoretical Framework for the Emergence of Tyrannical Behavioural Regimes:

Integrating Developmental, Institutional, and Evolutionary Models (Pre‑1914)


Ouadi Maakoul



Abstract

This dissertation develops a purely theoretical, falsifiable framework for understanding why particular rulers, under comparable macro‑institutional conditions, go on to produce regimes marked by extreme power concentration, extreme human cost, high administrative capacity, and low public goods provision. It synthesises developmental adversity research, personality psychology, evolutionary leadership theory, and the political economy of autocracy, while explicitly rejecting genetic determinism, clinical labelling, and cultural anachronism.

The central claim is that tyrannical behavioural regimes become probabilistically more likely under a specific conjunction of developmental, social, institutional, and contextual conditions. The model treats harshness and unpredictability as independent dimensions of developmental adversity without invoking the contested fast‑slow life‑history calibration. Instead, adversity is theorised to shape threat sensitivity, planning horizons, and interpersonal trust—mechanisms firmly anchored in general developmental science. The dominance–prestige typology from evolutionary leadership theory is restricted to the face‑to‑face elite environment where it is empirically defensible, and it is used solely to provide discriminant validity for the tyrannical regime class.

All constructs are translated into historically defensible proxy variables. Familial Temperamental Clustering (FTC) captures the social‑developmental aggregation of extreme behavioural tendencies within ruling families. Developmental Adversity Deviation (DAD) evaluates childhood harshness and unpredictability as deviations from polity‑period‑specific norms. Pre‑accession behavioural indicators are coded exclusively from sources composed before accession, using a Multi‑Genre Source Triangulation protocol (MGST) that draws on Rankean source criticism. A Historical Source Audit Log (HSAL) ensures digital traceability, and a Historical Evidence Reliability Index (HERI) with an explicit aggregation rule weights observations by evidential quality.

The dissertation establishes a complete methodological architecture—including formal theoretical expectations, a pre‑registration template, and explicit scope conditions—designed to pre‑commit future comparative‑historical research. By bridging idiographic historical biography and nomothetic political science without biological or psychological reductionism, it lays the epistemological foundation for a cumulative research programme on the developmental and institutional origins of destructive political rule.

