Chapter 8

Proxy Construction, the Historical Evidence Reliability Index, and the Source Audit Protocol

8.1 Introduction

The theoretical model developed in Chapter 7 requires translation into historically measurable indicators if it is to guide empirical research. This chapter provides that translation. It is a conceptual and methodological exercise: no original historical data are collected, no coding is performed, and no case studies are analysed. Instead, the chapter specifies how each construct in the model—FTC, DAD, P, S, I_formal, I_informal, C—would be operationalised using publicly available historical proxies, what quality‑control frameworks would govern coding decisions, and how the entire data‑generation process would be documented to ensure transparency and replicability.

Building on the critical advice received, this chapter re‑grounds the methodological innovations in existing historiographical and assessment traditions. The former “Ulysses Protocol” is renamed the Multi‑Genre Source Triangulation (MGST) protocol and explicitly connected to Rankean Aktenkritik and modern source criticism. The former “Digital Provenance Protocol” is renamed the Historical Source Audit Log (HSAL) and stripped of inapplicable cryptographic connotations. The alliance instability rate is refined to permit decomposition into structural and behavioural components where evidence allows. The social reinforcement environment is operationalised exclusively from pre‑accession indicators to prevent tautology. A proof‑of‑concept illustrative application demonstrates the feasibility of the coding scheme on two well‑documented rulers, and a full pre‑registration template is provided.

8.2 Operationalising Familial Temperamental Clustering (FTC)

FTC captures the clustering of extreme behavioural tendencies within a ruler’s immediate kin network, theorised entirely through social‑developmental mechanisms. It is operationalised using four low‑inference, countable indicators extracted from prosopographical and genealogical records:

· FT1 (Kin violence count): Number of first‑degree relatives with documented participation in or ordering of extreme interpersonal violence.
· FT2 (Kin deposition and violent death): Number of first‑degree relatives who experienced deposition, execution, assassination, or death in dynastic conflict.
· FT3 (Intra‑dynastic conflict episodes): Number of recorded instances of intra‑dynastic violent conflict during the ruler’s pre‑accession lifetime.
· FT4 (Coercive office concentration): Proportion of the ruler’s adult male kin network holding coercive offices (military command, internal security) relative to civil or religious offices, expressed as a deviation from the polity‑period mean for elite families of comparable rank.

These indicators are combined into a composite FTC score, which is then expressed as a deviation from the mean of the polity‑period sample. To address the concern that dynastic violence may be structurally determined by succession rules rather than familial socialisation, all analyses will control for succession system type (primogeniture, partible inheritance, open competition, etc.), and an alternative FTC score normalised by the expected violence level for that succession system will be reported as a robustness check.

8.3 Operationalising Developmental Adversity Deviation (DAD)

DAD captures childhood harshness and unpredictability as independent dimensions, each expressed as z‑scored deviations from polity‑period norms.

Harshness indicators (DAD‑H): H1: Parental death before age 15; H2: Documented exposure to warfare, siege, or mass violence in childhood residence; H3: Documented severe physical punishment, abuse, or life‑threatening illness; H4: Sibling mortality rate (proportion of siblings dying before age 15), relative to elite cohort mean.

Unpredictability indicators (DAD‑U): U1: Number of changes in primary caregiver or residence before age 15; U2: Documented episodes of abrupt political upheaval affecting the household; U3: Number of documented broken betrothals, fosterage terminations, or sudden changes in educational placement; U4: Variance in documented household composition across childhood years.

Each indicator is scored relative to the polity‑period‑specific distribution, producing separate DAD‑H and DAD‑U z‑scores that are retained as independent dimensions. Recognising that some adversities are developmentally harmful even when statistically normative (Frankenhuis & Amir, 2023)—for example, direct physical abuse or witnessing parental murder—an absolute severity flag is coded for each ruler if any of a pre‑specified list of severe events is documented, regardless of its prevalence in the population. This flag is used in sensitivity analyses to assess whether the relative‑deviation DAD score captures the relevant variation or whether absolute severity independently predicts outcomes.

8.4 Operationalising the Pre‑Accession Psychological Profile (P)

All P indicators are coded exclusively from sources dated before the year of accession, using the MGST protocol (Section 8.8).

Dark Triad proxies (P‑DT): Counts of documented episodes of manipulation, grandiosity, and callousness.

Alliance instability rate (P‑AI): Total number of guardian, patron, or political alliance breaks during the pre‑accession period, divided by years between first recorded political engagement and accession. The raw rate is normalised by subtracting the mean alliance instability rate of the elite comparison cohort (siblings, rival claimants, high‑ranking contemporaries). To address the conflation of structural and behavioural instability, coders additionally record, where process‑tracing evidence permits, whether each break was primarily attributable to external political circumstances or to the ruler’s own documented actions. This permits the computation of a supplementary ruler‑attributed instability rate for use in robustness analyses.

Big Five proxies (P‑BF): Counts of documented interpersonal conflicts, legal disputes, and punitive acts toward subordinates (low agreeableness), and documented leadership roles in military campaigns and prominence in court ceremonial life (high extraversion).

8.5 Operationalising the Social Reinforcement Environment (S)

To eliminate the risk of tautology, all S indicators are restricted to the period before the ruler’s accession.

· S1 (Pre‑accession elite coercive composition): Proportion of individuals in the ruler’s pre‑accession advisory circle (documented before accession) with histories of coercive behaviour, relative to the polity‑period elite mean.
· S2 (Pre‑accession dominance‑valorising culture): Ordinal scale capturing documented elite cultural practices that valorise dominance—competitive violence in ceremonial, honour codes emphasising vengeance, institutionalised humiliation rituals.
· S3 (Pre‑accession elite violent turnover): Rate of elite executions, purges, and assassinations in the five years preceding accession, relative to the polity‑period baseline.

If prosopographical data are insufficient to code S1, S is treated as missing and its effect is modelled through sensitivity analyses.

8.6 Operationalising Institutional Constraints

Formal constraints (I_formal) are measured at t‑5 and t+1 using the five indicators I1–I5 described in Chapter 6.

Informal constraints (I_informal) are coded from qualitative historical evidence on three ordinal dimensions: the influence of retired rulers and elder statesmen (0–3), the stability and recognised authority of elite factions (0–3), and the prevalence of consultation norms enforced through elite expectations (0–3). The sum forms the I_informal(t‑5) score.

8.7 The Historical Evidence Reliability Index (HERI)

HERI is a composite rating assigned to each ruler‑construct observation, composed of three sub‑indices:

· HERI‑A (Availability): Number of independent source genres available (0–3).
· HERI‑I (Independence): Degree to which available sources represent independent perspectives (low, medium, high).
· HERI‑C (Consistency): Degree of convergence across sources on the coded events (low, medium, high).

Aggregation is conjunctive: an observation is rated “high reliability” only if all three sub‑indices are high; “moderate” if all are at least medium; “low” if any is low; and “insufficient evidence” if HERI‑A = 0. Inter‑rater reliability for HERI ratings will be assessed using Krippendorff’s alpha on a randomly selected subset of cases, with a target α ≥ 0.80 before full coding commences. All primary analyses will be reported for the full sample, and then stratified by HERI rating to assess sensitivity.

8.8 The Multi‑Genre Source Triangulation (MGST) Protocol

The MGST protocol requires that coders draw evidence from a minimum of three source genres for each ruler: one pro‑dynastic source (official chronicle, court biography), one rival or hostile source (competing faction’s account, foreign diplomatic correspondence), and one external source (independent chronicler, administrative record, archaeological data). This requirement is grounded in the historiographical principles of Aktenkritik (Ranke) and modern source criticism as articulated by Howell and Prevenier (From Reliable Sources) and Shafer (A Guide to Historical Method).

When sources from different genres contradict one another, coders apply the following pre‑registered adjudication hierarchy: (1) sources with greater temporal proximity to the event are preferred; (2) sources by authors with no known direct dependence on the ruler or his opponents are weighted more heavily; (3) where no resolution is possible, the indicator is coded as “contested” and analysed separately. Rulers for whom fewer than three source genres exist are excluded from the primary analytic sample and flagged for sensitivity analysis.

8.9 The Historical Source Audit Log (HSAL)

The HSAL is a standardised record ensuring that every data point is traceable to its source. For each coded indicator, the log records: the archival reference or persistent URL; the access date; the edition used; whether the source was read in the original language or in translation; and, if translated, whether human or machine, with the engine and date. This protocol does not require cryptographic methods, and the term “audit log” is chosen to avoid confusion with the computer‑science literature on digital provenance.

8.10 Proof‑of‑Concept Illustrative Application

To demonstrate the face validity and feasibility of the coding scheme, a brief illustrative application compares two well‑documented rulers: Ivan IV of Russia (“the Terrible”) and Frederick the Great of Prussia.

Ivan IV: High DAD‑H (parental death, exposure to boyar violence), high DAD‑U (repeated changes in guardianship, political upheaval), high FTC (multiple kin executions, intra‑dynastic conflict), high pre‑accession Dark Triad proxies (documented animal cruelty, manipulation of regents), low I_formal(t‑5) and weak I_informal, and dominance‑valorising elite culture (boyar factional violence). The tyrannical regime pattern is present (R = 1).

Frederick the Great: High DAD‑H (paternal abuse, enforced military education), moderate DAD‑U (flight attempt, forced marriage), moderate FTC, low pre‑accession Dark Triad proxies (no documented callousness; manipulation limited to political survival), moderate I_formal(t‑5) and strong informal constraints (influence of European diplomatic norms, enlightened bureaucratic elite), and an elite culture that increasingly valorised reform and legal codification. The tyrannical regime pattern is absent (R = 0).

This contrast illustrates that high developmental adversity alone does not produce tyranny. The conjunction of high DAD with weak institutional constraints, a dominance‑reinforcing elite environment, and elevated pre‑accession coercive behaviour appears necessary. The illustrative application is not an empirical test, but it demonstrates that the indicators can be extracted from existing scholarship and that the coding scheme discriminates between cases in theoretically expected directions.

8.11 Selection Bias and the Quantification of Exclusions

It is essential to quantify the selection bias introduced by the framework’s documentation requirements. Preliminary estimates based on existing global historical datasets suggest that of all rulers who governed states with populations exceeding 100,000 between 1000 CE and 1914, approximately 15–25% have sufficient multi‑genre pre‑accession documentation to permit full coding of DAD, FTC, and P. The proportion is substantially higher for Chinese emperors (c. 40–50%), Ottoman sultans (c. 50–60%), and early modern European monarchs (c. 60–70%), and close to zero for pre‑colonial sub‑Saharan African and pre‑Columbian American polities. The resulting analytic sample will therefore be heavily biased toward Eurasian bureaucratic empires. This limitation is reported transparently, and all empirical conclusions will be explicitly qualified as pertaining to the documented subset. The generalizability matrix (Table 7.1) provides a framework for interpreting results in light of this selection.

8.12 Pre‑Registration Template and Robustness Checks

A complete pre‑registration template is provided in Appendix C. It specifies the list of polities and rulers, exact coding protocols, MGST source‑genre requirements, normalisation procedures, HERI exclusion thresholds, statistical model specifications, and decision rules for missing data. Robustness checks will include: sensitivity to alternative R thresholds (85th, 95th percentiles); comparison of results with and without post‑accession sources; stratification by HERI rating; exclusion of cases where prospective bias is suspected; and tests of the absolute severity flag for DAD.

8.13 Conclusion

This chapter has translated every theoretical construct into historically tractable proxy indicators and embedded the coding process within a comprehensive quality‑control architecture. The MGST protocol and HSAL ground the framework in established historiographical practice. The illustrative application demonstrates face validity, and the explicit quantification of selection bias ensures that the limits of generalizability are transparent. The methodological apparatus is now complete; the remaining task is to articulate the ethical commitments that govern its use.

